The Bombieri--Vinogradov theorem \cite{bombieri1965large,vinogradov1965density} famously states that for any $x > 2$, $A > 0$, and $B$ sufficiently large in terms of $A$, one has
\begin{equation} \label{eq:bv}
    \sum_{q \le x^{1/2}/(\log x)^B} \sup_{(a, q) = 1} 
    \left\vert \pi(x; q, a) - \frac{\pi(x)}{\varphi(q)} \right\vert 
    \ll_A 
    \frac{x}{(\log x)^A},
\end{equation}
where $\pi(x)$ denotes the number of primes up to $x$, and $\pi(x; q, a)$ denotes the number of these primes which are congruent to $a$ modulo $q$.

\begin{equation} \label{eq:bv}
