where $r_0 := te_0 (h_1e_1'\ell_2 - h_2e_2'\ell_1)\overline{b \ell_1 \ell_2}$ (defined mod $k$), and $\mu = \mu(\ell_1, \ell_2, t, e_0, e_1', e_2', d)$ is the unique solution (mod $te_0e_1'e_2'$) to the congruences $\mu \equiv d\overline{\ell_1} \pmod{te_0e_1'}$ and $\mu \equiv d\overline{\ell_2} \pmod{te_0e_2'}$; the latter is well-defined by the Chinese Remainder Theorem, since $(te_0e_1', te_0e_2') = te_0(e_1', e_2') = te_0$, $[te_0e_1', te_0e_2'] = te_0e_1'e_2'$, and $d\overline{\ell_1} \equiv d\overline{\ell_2} \pmod{te_0}$. Crucially, note that $\mu$ does not depend on $k$.

We can thus complete Kloosterman sums using \cref{lem:completion}, with
\begin{gather*} 
    q := te_0e_1'e_2',  \\
    J_0 := 32\, x^\eta \frac{KTE^2}{NE_0} \ge x^\eta \frac{kq}{N},
